From cb657bf68afee800c2a5c7fb52debb26c378afee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chromium Extend <noreply@localhost>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 20:12:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 57/58] Keep Manifest V2 extensions working

Chromium finished removing Manifest V2, and with it the whole deprecation
experiment: ManifestV2ExperimentManager, ExtensionManifestV2Availability,
AllowLegacyMV2Extensions and the enterprise policy that used to grant a
reprieve are all gone. What remains is a single hardcoded return in
ShouldDisableLegacyExtensions(), with no flag, policy or build argument
behind it.

That one function feeds every enforcement point, so returning false restores
them together: installation is no longer refused, MustRemainDisabled stops
forcing an installed extension back off, and the startup sweep stops
disabling what survived.

Nothing else was needed to make content blockers work. The
webRequestBlocking permission is still declared for any MV2 extension with no
restriction on where it came from, and the background page, webRequest and
content script machinery are all compiled in this configuration already.
---
 extensions/browser/manifest_v2_handler.cc | 22 +++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/extensions/browser/manifest_v2_handler.cc b/extensions/browser/manifest_v2_handler.cc
index 5841d3dfbc..e58b1f5eb5 100644
--- a/extensions/browser/manifest_v2_handler.cc
+++ b/extensions/browser/manifest_v2_handler.cc
@@ -114,7 +114,27 @@ bool ShouldDisableLegacyExtensions() {
     return false;
   }
 
-  return true;
+  // Bare: keep Manifest V2 working.
+  //
+  // Upstream returns true unconditionally here. There is no longer a flag, a
+  // policy or an enterprise escape hatch: the whole deprecation experiment
+  // (ManifestV2ExperimentManager, ExtensionManifestV2Availability and the rest)
+  // was deleted once the removal completed, leaving this one hardcoded return.
+  //
+  // It is the only input to all three enforcement points, so returning false
+  // restores the lot together: ShouldBlockExtensionInstallation lets an MV2
+  // extension install, ShouldBlockExtensionEnable stops
+  // StandardManagementPolicyProvider::MustRemainDisabled from forcing it back
+  // off, and DisableAffectedExtensions stops sweeping it away at startup.
+  //
+  // Nothing else has to change for content blockers to work. The
+  // webRequestBlocking permission is still declared for any MV2 extension with
+  // no location restriction, and the background page, webRequest and content
+  // script machinery all compile in this configuration already.
+  //
+  // This matches what Brave does, which injects the same early return into this
+  // same function.
+  return false;
 }
 
 }  // namespace
